    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Assignment vs Equality:</strong> Confusion between = (assignment) and == (comparison).</li>
            <li><strong>Global vs Local Scope:</strong> Believing a variable declared inside a function is accessible everywhere.</li>
            <li><strong>Data Types:</strong> Assuming Python doesn't have data types because it's dynamically typed (students must understand that 5 and "5" are processed differently).</li>
            <li><strong>Errors:</strong> Believing a program that runs has no errors (ignoring logic errors).</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Language and Meaning:</strong> Why is natural language (English) unsuitable for programming? Ambiguity vs the rigid syntax required for machine logic.</li>
                <li><strong>Objective Truth:</strong> Is a computer program's output always "true" if the logic is flawed? "Garbage in, Garbage out."</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

